# statements and control structures

- if, else, elsif -- as usual, delimited with end, last expression of a structure is returned
- if can be used as a modifier: puts "bla" if condition..., also elvis operator x==1 ? "true" : "false"
- unless -- opposite of if
- case -- like switch:

case x
  when condition
    expression
  when ...
  else
    ...
  end
end

## Loops:

- either by statements like while, until, of for x in collection, or by custom looping constructs called iterators (more common, e.g. .times, .map, .each, .upto, .collect .select, .reject, .inject...)
- Objects have iterator methods that accept *blocks* that form the body of the loop
- these are internal iterators (the iterator controls the loop, not external iterators where the client code controls the iteration) -- you can write custom iterator methods using yield keyword

Iterating enumerables:
(5..7).each {|x| print x}
(5..7).each_with_index {|x,i| print x,i}

Searching, reducing
a.count {|x| x % 2 == 1}      # count odd elements 
a.find_all {|x| x % 2 == 1}   # return odd elements 
a.reject {|x| x % 2 == 1}     # return all but odd elements 
a.partition {|x| x % 2 == 1}  # return odd and even elements in separate arrays 

a.inject {|total, x| total+x} # general purpose reduce functions, synonym "reduce", 
                              # block takes 2 args, accumulated value and iterated one
a.reduce(:+)  # same as ^

## Blocks

- delimited either by {} (one line) or do/end (multiple lines)
- have own variable scope but can access variables defined outside of block
- you can pass params to a block the same as to any method, *param collects extra params to an array, unlike methods, blocks don't have names and can be only invoked trough iterators

## Altering control flow:

- return -- causes the method to exit and return to its caller, when used in block, the enclosing method of that block exits and returns, not just the block itself
- break -- causes the loop or iterator to exit, when used in block it breaks out from the block to the next statement after the invoking iterator of the block
- next -- causes the loop or iterator to skip the rest of the current iteration and move to the next one
- redo -- restarts the current iteration of a loop or iterator
- throw/catch -- multilevel break -- throw breaks out to whatever level catch is placed

## Exception handling

- begin -- like try in pyton
- raise -- raising exception
- rescue -- like catch in python
- else -- executed if code in begin statement runs to completion without exception
- ensure -- like finally in python
